\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {One message, two legs, and the two places a latency fails.} (a)~Where everything runs. The producer and consumer processes share a client machine and its one clock, and the broker runs on a second machine. Leg~1 carries a message from the producer to the broker, which writes it to its log and returns an acknowledgment along leg~1. Leg~2 carries the message on to the consumer. The publish latency $A$ covers leg~1 out and back, and the end-to-end latency $D$ covers leg~1 and then leg~2. Failure~1, a late read of $t_{\mathrm  {ack}}$, enters at the producer's I/O thread. Failure~2 enters where a tool reports $D$. Its clock moves in steps of $\tau $, so a message faster than one step can read as zero, and a filter that keeps only values above zero deletes it uncounted. (b)~When each timestamp is read, on one clock. The producer's application thread takes $t_{\mathrm  {sched}}$, when the message is due, and $t_{\mathrm  {pub}}$, at the publish call. Its client library's I/O thread takes $t_{\mathrm  {ack}}$ only after waiting $\delta _{\mathrm  {ack}}$ for a core. The consumer takes $t_{\mathrm  {recv}}$ after its own wait $\delta _{\mathrm  {recv}}$, and $t_{\mathrm  {out}}$ once it has handled the message. Running from $t_{\mathrm  {sched}}$ to $t_{\mathrm  {out}}$, $E$ is $D$ plus the wait before the publish call and the consumer's own work. Here $A$ runs past $D$, so $S = t_{\mathrm  {recv}} - t_{\mathrm  {ack}} = D - A$ is negative although nothing arrived early. The path drawn is Kafka{}'s. Redis{} timestamps the acknowledgment on the application thread and goes negative anyway, because that thread also waits for a core.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:model}{{1}{2}{\textbf {One message, two legs, and the two places a latency fails.} (a)~Where everything runs. The producer and consumer processes share a client machine and its one clock, and the broker runs on a second machine. Leg~1 carries a message from the producer to the broker, which writes it to its log and returns an acknowledgment along leg~1. Leg~2 carries the message on to the consumer. The publish latency $A$ covers leg~1 out and back, and the end-to-end latency $D$ covers leg~1 and then leg~2. Failure~1, a late read of $t_{\mathrm {ack}}$, enters at the producer's I/O thread. Failure~2 enters where a tool reports $D$. Its clock moves in steps of $\tau $, so a message faster than one step can read as zero, and a filter that keeps only values above zero deletes it uncounted. (b)~When each timestamp is read, on one clock. The producer's application thread takes $t_{\mathrm {sched}}$, when the message is due, and $t_{\mathrm {pub}}$, at the publish call. Its client library's I/O thread takes $t_{\mathrm {ack}}$ only after waiting $\delta _{\mathrm {ack}}$ for a core. The consumer takes $t_{\mathrm {recv}}$ after its own wait $\delta _{\mathrm {recv}}$, and $t_{\mathrm {out}}$ once it has handled the message. Running from $t_{\mathrm {sched}}$ to $t_{\mathrm {out}}$, $E$ is $D$ plus the wait before the publish call and the consumer's own work. Here $A$ runs past $D$, so $S = t_{\mathrm {recv}} - t_{\mathrm {ack}} = D - A$ is negative although nothing arrived early. The path drawn is \kafka {}'s. \redis {} timestamps the acknowledgment on the application thread and goes negative anyway, because that thread also waits for a core}{figure.1}{}}
