\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {What the remedies buy, measured.} (a)~The error of a latency timed from the acknowledgment on each of our $70$ conditions (crosses), and the error once the publish latency is added back (dots), against the condition's end-to-end latency. The curve is the error $A/D$ at the median publish latency. On the dashed line the publish latency equals the end-to-end latency, and the curve meets it at $0.72$~ms. Across conditions $A$ runs $500$--$1900\,\mu $s between its tenth and ninetieth percentiles, shown as the band, which puts the error at $10$~ms at $5$--$19\%$ and the crossover at $0.50$--$1.90$~ms. The shaded decade holds the sub-millisecond medians now reported for brokers. (b)~The negative-span rate of each matched pair at normal priority (circles) and at real-time priority (squares), at its own host utilization. Every pair falls, with disjoint intervals.}}{9}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:exposure}{{4}{9}{\textbf {What the remedies buy, measured.} (a)~The error of a latency timed from the acknowledgment on each of our $\spanRatioConditions $ conditions (crosses), and the error once the publish latency is added back (dots), against the condition's end-to-end latency. The curve is the error $A/D$ at the median publish latency. On the dashed line the publish latency equals the end-to-end latency, and the curve meets it at $\exposureCrossover $~ms. Across conditions $A$ runs $\exposureLagLo $--$\exposureLagHi \,\mu $s between its tenth and ninetieth percentiles, shown as the band, which puts the error at $10$~ms at $\exposureErrTenLo $--$\exposureErrTenHi \%$ and the crossover at $\exposureCrossoverLo $--$\exposureCrossoverHi $~ms. The shaded decade holds the sub-millisecond medians now reported for brokers. (b)~The negative-span rate of each matched pair at normal priority (circles) and at real-time priority (squares), at its own host utilization. Every pair falls, with disjoint intervals}{figure.4}{}}
